  "provider_query": "Jargon File entry that defines yak shaving",
  "answer": "**Jargon File entry (yak shaving)**\n\nFrom [The Jargon File](http://catb.org/jargon/html/Y/yak-shaving.html) (catb.org, entry dated in the file around late 2003):\n\n> **yak shaving**  \n> [MIT AI Lab, after 2000: orig. probably from a Ren & Stimpy episode.] Any seemingly pointless activity which is actually necessary to solve a problem which solves a problem which, several levels of recursion later, solves the real problem you're working on.\n\n### Relevant results\n\n1. **Jargon File (canonical)** — Full short entry as above. Source: http://catb.org/jargon/html/Y/yak-shaving.html\n\n2. **ZVON index** — Points to the Jargon File glossary entry for “yak shaving” as hacker slang. Source: https://zvon.org/comp/m/yak_shaving.html\n\n3. **TechTarget** — Programming slang for an endless series of small tasks required before the next project step; credits Carlin J. Vieri (MIT, 1990s) and *Ren & Stimpy*’s “Yak Shaving Day.” Source: https://www.techtarget.com/whatis/definition/yak-shaving\n\n4. **Levellers.ai** — Traces coinage to Carlin J. Vieri at the MIT AI Lab (1990s), inspired by *Ren & Stimpy*; notes Jeremy H. Brown’s Feb 2000 MIT mailing-list definition and the Jargon File’s later recording of the term. Source: https://www.levellers.ai/what-is/yak-shaving\n\n5. **DEV Community** — Means necessary but seemingly unrelated subtasks on the way to the real task (e.g., dependency/tooling chains). Source: https://dev.to/cerchie/tech-jargon-defined-what-is-yak-shaving-3hj8\n\n6. **ITBroker glossary** — Informal engineering slang for a recursive chain of prerequisite tasks before the intended work. Source: https://www.itbroker.com/resources/glossary/yak-shaving\n\n7. **Visual Thesaurus** — “Annoying succession of tasks” needed to reach the actual goal; cites MIT/Vieri and the Jargon File’s *Ren & Stimpy* link. Source: https://www.visualthesaurus.com/cm/wc/heavy-lifting-and-shaving-yaks-corporate-lingo/\n\n8. **PCMag** — Weaker/alternate gloss (trivial tasks or getting sidetracked); gives a different, less accepted origin (“Quest for the Shaven Yak”). Not aligned with the Jargon File. Source: https://www.pcmag.com/encyclopedia/term/yak-shaving",
